pecies to benefit applied  peanut breeders in the development  selection and release of new cultivars  this phase was completed in 2019 with  the release of the arachisteronensis  hippiensis and hypogeogenomes  in phase 2 of the peanut genome  initiative the peanut research  foundation is funding four pillars of  applied research in an effort to utilize  the reference genomes produced in phase  one to develop germplasm and cultivar  releases for peanut improvement  the areas of interest under the phase ii  directive is improvement of disease  resistance aflatoxin research both pre  and post harvest and storage  drought tolerance and flavor improvement  for this research we fall under disease  resistance as we're trying to improve  and release cultivars with higher levels  of leaf spot resistance which was their  main emphasis for this category  within our breeding program we have  varying levels of resistance depending  on the resources in the specific program  and the cultivated breeding program the  one that results in cultivar releases to  growers we have a wide range of  resistant and susceptible reading lines  on the left this is a comparison of the  older cultivar  ncva 92r  and a newly developed breeding line  n16021  relative to the performance in the  cultivated breeding program  the wild species breeding program has  way more resistant germplasm lines  on the right the line in the center is a  newly developed interspecific hybrid  between an older cultivar gregory and a  wild species diploid arachis diagoy  the level of resistance is greater than  any line in the cultivated breeding  program however the agronomic properties  are extremely low  on an overall view of the two breeding  programs there is clearly a difference  in the level of resistance to leaf spot  represented in these drone images  on the left is our advanced leaf spot  trial clearly a number of lines are  brown and completely defoliated under  intense leaf spot pressure with no  applications of fungicides on the right  the wild species shows consistently high  levels of resistance to leaf spot under  the same leaf spot pressure with no  applications of fungicides  these two images were taken on the same  day the main sources of resistance that  the cultivated breeding program is using  for early and late leaf spot resistance  comes from a ractus cardinal ci and  arrakis diagoy  in the development of the cultivar  bailey a wild species introgression line  from miractus carnacion was used to  develop  no3o81t which eventually became bailey  so we know that it's possible to  transfer these beneficial resistance  genes into superior yielding lines in  the cultivated breeding program  currently our efforts to identify and  select for leaf spot resistance are  somewhat limited to a subjective  evaluation of breeding plots by the  peanut breeder  these ratings aren't as straightforward  as you'd think and often can be  compromised by changes in weather  raters replications field size etc  for leaf spot we rate on a one to nine  scale where one means the line is  resistant or doesn't have any visual  lesions or defoliation  5 means it has significant leaf spot  lesions but no noticeable defoliation  and then 9 which means the plot has been  completely defoliated or reduced to  vines  in the image to the right i have a  peanut leaf  that shows symptoms of early and late  leaf spot  early leaf spot is depicted as having  light brown lesions with a yellow ring  or halo however the halo isn't always  present  late leaf spot are more darkly colored  and generally lack a halo with prominent  sporulation on the underside of the leaf  surface  when taking ratings on the plot level  it's incredibly difficult separating out  early versus late leaf spot percentages  visually therefore our ratings simply  become an overall percentage of lesions  when it comes to taking ratings on plots  typically the ratings are relative to  other plots  when i take a rating on leaf spot i  usually walk the entire field and get a  mental representation of what i would  consider to be a five rating  i then continue looking at the plots  until i'm confident i could identify all  ranges of the rating scale  i'll give you an example here i'm  suggesting the plot on the far right is  considered a five with only knowing this  rating could the ratings of the other  plots be identified  here are my thoughts  based on the rating of a five i might  rate this plot a three in comparison the  plot is healthy and had minor symptoms  of leaf spot  the one next to it is nearly completely  defoliated so i'll rate it in eight  the final two plots i would rate a four  because of a slightly higher leaf spot  lesion percentage and the one in the  middle the arrakis diagonal line i would  probably rate a one because it has no  visual symptoms of leaf spot and would  be considered resistant  the rating may differ due to changes in  weather between raiders or across the  field because we're human  in order to get a consistent  representation of leaf spot infection a  high throughput method of evaluation  needs to be developed  the overall importance of taking good  consistent ratings is that it helps us  to map the genes responsible for  resistance so that we can design genetic  markers that will identify families and  or lines with those important genes and  then use them in our program to make  selections on the left is a  representation of a single  introgression block and multiplexed casp  or pace assay that we use for selection  purposes within our program  for a single assay we can select for  fixation in green or enrichment yellow  for the next generation of development  for an integration block we design  flanking markers with similar  fluorescence patterns after the same  number of pcr cycles to bring  intragression blocks conferring  resistance from the wild species program  into the cultivated program and finally  we can stack multiple single marker  assays together with similar  fluorescence patterns after the same  number of pcr cycles to select for  multiple traits  on the right is a simple schematic of  the ncsu peanut breeding and genetics  programs breeding pipeline we make  crosses and we send those to puerto rico  to get an extra generation of  development when the lines come back we  progeny test them in the field for  disease resistance in this case leaf  spot resistance we identify the best  families from the field and pair it with  the marker results select the plants  with the best pods for those families  and then repeat the cycle after several  cycles these lines that advance to our  yield trials and hopefully one will be  selected for release  so based on this information we  developed a project with the following  objectives  the first is to identify objective  methods for evaluating leaf spot both  early and late leaf spot for improving  phenotyping and data quality  the second is to produce a high  throughput protocol for objective  methods for evaluating leaf spot  the third is to evaluate and genetically  map early and late leaf spot resistance  among the breeding families and lines in  the north carolina state university  peanut breeding and genetics program  and finally use these identified genetic  markers to select families and lines in  the cultivar development program with  increased resistance to leaf spot and  this can be through marker assisted back  crossing or selection for the purposes  of this presentation we'll be reporting  results for the first objective only as  this is an ongoing project  the data that i'm going to show today  represents some of the preliminary work  that was conducted in 2019 and 2020  in 2019 representative samples of  commonly grown cultivars within the  breeding program leaf spot trial were  assessed using developed scripts in fiji  which is just image j to separate the  visual rating into their sub components  so what we wanted to know is one could  we effectively scan the tissue collected  and differentiate between early and late  leaf spot using the images alone and in  addition  could we then use the tissue scans to  estimate an overall percentage of leaf  spot infections on leaf tissue and then  two  could we track using uav imaging over  time the relative canopy loss due to  leaf spot infection that could be  attributable to defoliation  to do this work eight leaves were  randomly selected per plot and then the  32 leaflets were scanned on an epson  scanner for analysis  the canopy cover was captured using a  dji mavic 2 pro drone which comes  stocked with a one inch sensor and  images were stitched together using  agisoft's meta shape  so it turns out it's way more difficult  to separate these two diseases using  basic thresholding  the issues include one whether each leaf  spot can be accurately identified and  how long it takes to go through each  leaf sample  two the resolution in the image may not  allow us to accurately differentiate the  spots  three the thresholding cutoffs that sort  the clusters leave spots  may be too similar  and four the population pressure of each  disease in a given year may change for  example the peanut pathologist here  estimated that late leaf spot infection  was around 95 percent in 2019 among the  two diseases  in order to attempt at differentiating  these diseases  more data collected on the specific leaf  spots is needed in order to develop a  better training model for accurately  classifying early and late leaf spot  that being said getting a percentage of  lesion areas pretty straightforward  the fiji script is designed to threshold  the leaf spots and the entirety of the  leaf surface separately  the ratio between these two pixel totals  gives us our leaf spot estimates on the  leaf surface  the script is automated to run through  each of the images collected per plot  and can calculate the final leaf spot  percentage  similarly the drone images help us to  estimate canopy cover changes during  heavy leaf spot pressure  the top and the bottom rows of images  represent pre and post leaf spot onset  respectively in the top row images were  taken on september 6th and in the bottom  row images were taken on october 8th  prior to harvest the first column shows  the thresholding across all of the plots  the second column shows a similar image  crop to our gridding of each individual  plot and the final column shows the  final image threshold  using these two canopy scores we can  estimate a change in canopy cover that  we're calling defoliation  in some instances when a plot is  relatively unaffected by leaf spot the  defoliation score can actually be  negative due to the continual or  unaffected growth  we can then apply this method to our  entire breeding program in the field we  grew out 220 lines in 2020 and used this  approach to get leaf tissue and  defoliation estimates for each plot on a  bi-weekly basis during heavy leaf spot  pressure  we additionally have yield data  collected on these untreated plots  along with plots that were treated with  fungicide but this data will not be  presented here  presented are the results obtained using  the previously described methods  collected in 2020 on the 220 breeding  and germplasm line selected from the  program  on the left is a figure of 26 lines that  were whole genome sequence for another  project for developing a high throughput  genotyping platform for our program  moving forward  these lines were chosen based on several  traits of interest including resistance  to leaf spot  these differences in the bar chart  represents the visual ratings taken on  these genotypes prior to harvest  on the right are some basic statistics  describing the different evaluation  methods including the tissue scans alone  the drone or defoliation score alone or  a combination of the two using the first  principal component when compared to the  visual scores  in each instance the evaluation type was  highly correlated with the visual rating  in addition the visual score which again  is a one to nine rating  was also modeled using logistic  regression to determine the predictive  potential of each evaluation type  the logistic model produces overall  concordance scores which is the model's  predictive potential  and odds ratios for each evaluation  parameter  the concordance scores compares pairwise  scores across the data set to determine  if the probabilities associated with  being in a higher visual score are true  for each pair paired test  for the odds ratio this is determining  the odds of being in a higher visual  score given an increase in the  evaluation unit  if you look at the models evaluated  tissue alone drone alone pc1 and the  full model of tissue plus drone there is  an increase in concordance when  considering more parameters  but it seems the pc one that explains  around 79.1 percent of the variation is  similar to the full model  that being said there isn't much of a  difference in concordance when using  tissue or defoliation data alone either  the odds increase  with the number of parameters too  for tissue alone there is 1.3 times the  odds of being in a higher visual score  when the percentage of leaf spot surface  area increases which makes sense  under the pc-1 were 10 times more likely  to be in a higher visual score when the  two components tissue and drone increase  together  our future work on this leaf spot  evaluation method is funded through the  usda nifa plant breeding program  and the work over the next two field  seasons will be conducted by my phd  student cassie newman  the work that we're doing in the field  this year consists of multiple rating  systems timings and data collection  types i will be evaluating the plots for  leaf spot on the previous 1 and 9 scale  this will be done in a weekly manner  cassie will be evaluating the plots  using the low throughput objective  method of drone imaging for defoliation  and tissue collection for lesion counts  and leaf spot differentiation  lastly we'll also be collecting the high  throughput images and developing an  algorithm to rapidly analyze the images  for instant leaf spot ratings using cell  phone capture  the collection timing will essentially  be from august through harvest in  october  here we're diagramming our high  throughput method that just uses drone  and or cell phone images to rapidly take  ratings on both defoliation and leaf  spot lesions  the idea here is to evaluate the 220  lines in the field using drone imaging  and can currently walk through the plots  taking cell phone images the images will  be instantly uploaded where an image  software with a customized algorithm  would be waiting to analyze the plots  in addition we're going to evaluate  using canopio to determine the  effectiveness of this application in  detecting leaf spot lesion counts and  canopy changes  using these approaches ratings can be  objectively taken and in less time  if the analysis is completed i can then  look at the data and make selections  within these lines for further  development  ultimately what we're hoping to achieve  is to pair our newly developed  genotyping pipeline which capitalizes on  whole genome sequencing and in-silica  digestion for optimized gbs  developed by cassie newman to high  quality leaf spot ratings in order to  identify regions or inter introgression  blocks that are influencing leaf spot  resistance  with that i would like to thank all of  those organizations that sponsor our  research  particularly the national peanut board  the peanut research foundation  severn peanut company in hampton farms  and the usdars and nifa programs and i  want to thank you for your time  if you have any questions regarding this  project  please feel free to ask or you may call  or email me thank you

